**Read:** [Atomistic computer modeling of materials — MIT 3.320](../sources/Atomistic-Computer-Modeling-MIT.lingua#r_12NX0AYTQYSKNAS77BMZP9DQDH), free-energy methods; [Statistical Physics — David Tong](../sources/Statistical-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_7FMG47HD1MT706NT3CABRTFNS2), thermodynamic potentials; the earlier phase equilibrium and assessed-calculation sections.

Potential energy at a minimum is not a finite-temperature free energy. Entropy, accessible states and ensemble constraints matter. For a sufficiently sampled differentiable canonical family Hλ at fixed temperature, ∂F/∂λ=⟨∂Hλ/∂λ⟩λ. Integrating between a known reference and the target provides a free-energy difference when the path and sampling support the relation.

Numerical quadrature, imperfect equilibration and reference-state errors all contribute. Crossing a poorly sampled transition can invalidate a smooth-looking integral. Comparing phases requires compatible composition and thermodynamic constraints; coexistence conditions involve the appropriate chemical potentials or common tangents, not arbitrary energy zeros.

**Work:** Derive thermodynamic integration from the partition function. Verify it for a solvable classical model, then examine a course material example. Refine both the integration grid and sampling length, close a reversible cycle where possible, and separate hysteresis from equilibrium. Explain how the free-energy uncertainty affects a predicted phase boundary.

